<h2>A forty-year marriage reaches the Family Court</h2>
<p>The parties married in 1983 and have two adult children. The husband, a retired Army officer, left the matrimonial home in October 2019 and filed for divorce on the ground of cruelty under Section 13(1)(ia) of the HMA; the wife denied the allegations and, in 2019, filed complaints under Section 498A IPC and Section 12 of the DV Act. In the divorce proceedings she sought pendente lite maintenance and litigation expenses under Section 24. The Family Court dismissed that application in April 2022, holding her income at least equal to her husband\'s, and she appealed under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act, 1984.</p>

<h2>The financial picture before the Division Bench</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th></th><th>Wife (appellant)</th><th>Husband (respondent)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Recurring monthly income</td><td>About ₹69,000 (pension plus interest)</td><td>About ₹1,09,000 (pension)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Capital holdings</td><td>Retirement benefits exceeding ₹50 lakh</td><td>Investments of about ₹73 lakh and PPF of about ₹16.5 lakh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependants</td><td>—</td><td>Aged mother</td></tr>
</table>
<p>On these figures, the Bench held, the statutory threshold was simply not met: a spouse with a pension and investment returns of this order cannot be described as lacking independent income sufficient for her support. The appeal was dismissed with no order as to costs.</p>

<h2>Corpus is not income — the correction that matters</h2>
<p>Although the outcome went against the wife, the judgment\'s most useful contribution favours future claimants. The Family Court had effectively counted her ₹50 lakh retirement corpus against her, as though the capital itself were a monthly resource. The High Court refused to endorse that approach: retirement savings are accumulated capital built over a working life, and treating them as disposable income would force a retired litigant to consume her security to fund interim litigation. What enters the Section 24 calculus is the yield — interest, dividends, annuity payments — not the principal. Equally, the Bench rejected comparative income as the deciding factor; the inquiry is need-based, not parity-based.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance for retired litigants</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>For a claimant spouse: plead and prove actual monthly inflows, itemise expenses realistically, and expect each head of expenditure to be tested for reasonableness. For a responding spouse: disclose pension, investment returns and dependants fully — the court here gave weight to the husband\'s obligation towards his aged mother. For both: allegations of hidden business income must be supported by material showing current earnings; a defunct venture or charitable donations will not be converted into imputed income.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Section 24 operates only during the pendency of proceedings under the Hindu Marriage Act. Permanent alimony at or after decree is governed separately by Section 25, where the court\'s discretion is wider and capital assets can play a larger role in fixing a one-time settlement. The two inquiries should not be conflated.</p>
</div>
